    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d539">

                <pb facs="0658" n="590" sameAs="frus:frus1867p1/pg590"/>

                <head>Mr. Seward to Mr. Von
                        Baumbach.</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1863-11-13"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >November</hi> 13, 1863.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to return with my thanks
                    the accompaniment of your letter of the 24th of September last, which has been
                    read with much interest. The just and enlightened views expressed by the
                    Bavarian government in relation to the principles under which aliens in the
                    United States are under certain circumstances held liable to military duty are
                    very satisfactory.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <p rendition="#right">WILLIAM H. SEWARD.</p>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">L. Von Batjmbach,</hi> Esq., <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Consul of Bavaria, Milwaukee, Wisconsin</hi>.</p>
                <p>Pour copie conforme a l’original, Munich le 25 Septembre, 1867.</p>
                <p>Le Secretaire General au ministre Royal des Affaires Etrangères de Barière.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>D. BERTELI.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
